  },
  "key_facts": [
    {
      "fact": "AI crawlers made 5,227 requests to jsonhouse.com over 20 days while AI answer surfaces referred 7 human visitors, a ratio of 746.7 crawls per visit",
      "source": "jsonhouse original measurement, 2026-08-13",
      "category": "data"
    },
    {
      "fact": "ByteDance's Bytespider was the single largest crawler at 1,815 requests, 34.7% of all crawls, present on every one of the 20 days",
      "source": "jsonhouse original measurement, 2026-08-13",
      "category": "data"
    },
    {
      "fact": "459 requests (8.8% of all logged hits) wearing AI crawler names asked for credential paths such as .env and .git, which no documented AI crawler requests; this is a floor, not an estimate",
      "source": "jsonhouse original measurement, 2026-08-13",
      "category": "data"
    },
    {
      "fact": "On 2026-08-01 thirteen different vendors' bot names first appeared within a 30-millisecond window (11:25:09.531 to 11:25:09.561 UTC), evidence of one source cycling a user-agent list rather than thirteen independent crawlers",
      "source": "jsonhouse original measurement, 2026-08-13",
      "category": "case_study"
    },
    {
      "fact": "Answer-time fetches by ChatGPT-User, Claude-User, Perplexity-User and DuckAssistBot totalled 1,041 across 14 posts — 149 answer-time fetches for every referred human visit",
      "source": "jsonhouse original measurement, 2026-08-13",
      "category": "data"
    },
    {
      "fact": "The single most fetched page, the weekly LLM API pricing table, drew 503 answer-time fetches and was fetched on all 20 days of the window",
      "source": "jsonhouse original measurement, 2026-08-13",
      "category": "data"
    },
    {
      "fact": "Machine-readable endpoints returned 591 successful reads against 4,561 ordinary HTML page requests; /llms.txt itself was read 46 times by 5 distinct agents",
      "source": "jsonhouse original measurement, 2026-08-13",
      "category": "data"
    },
    {
      "fact": "Cloudflare's verified-bot classification, instrumented from 2026-08-09, is the only reliable discriminator between a real crawler and a forger; ASN is not, because vendor agents and arbitrary VMs share general-purpose cloud networks",
      "source": "jsonhouse original measurement, 2026-08-13",
      "category": "definition"
    },
    {
      "fact": "GoogleOther made 115 requests across 115 distinct paths on a single day with a 100% error rate — one request per path, all failing, the signature of path enumeration rather than crawling",
      "source": "jsonhouse original measurement, 2026-08-13",
      "category": "data"
    }
  ],
  "faq_summary": [
    {
      "q": "How much traffic do AI crawlers actually send back to a website?",
      "a": "On this site, almost none: 5,227 crawler requests against 7 referred human visits over 20 days. Referrals are undercounted because some clients strip the Referer header, so 7 is a floor."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does anything actually read llms.txt?",
      "a": "Yes, but rarely — 46 reads by 5 agents in 20 days, against 4,561 HTML page requests. Enough to say the file is not dead, not enough to call it a distribution channel."
    },
    {
      "q": "Can you tell if an AI crawler is faking its user agent?",
      "a": "Partly. The user-agent string is self-reported and trivially forged. Cloudflare's verified-bot classification checks the connection rather than the string. A behavioral floor also exists: 8.8% of requests asked for credential paths no real crawler requests."
    },
    {
      "q": "Is a crawl the same thing as being cited by an AI?",
      "a": "No. An indexing crawl means a page was collected. An answer-time fetch by ChatGPT-User, Claude-User or Perplexity-User means a page was pulled while an answer was being written — the closest observable proxy for citation. We logged 1,041 of those across 14 posts."
    }
  ],
